Rem ===================================================================
Rem cave-travel.shoddy -- how the rooms connect. Hand-edited source.
Rem First written by a generator from adventure.yaml; both are in
Rem obsolete/ now and neither is run. The C is the reference.
Rem
Rem The travel table, flattened one row per (rule, verb) pair.
Rem Row 0 is a dummy so that Tkey 0 can mean 'no travel here'.
Rem Tkey(loc) is the first row for a location; scan forward until
Rem a row with Stop set.
Rem ===================================================================

Rem main.c:719-723 folds cond_goto and cond_pct into one branch:
Rem
Rem     if (condarg1 == 0 || PCT(condarg1)) break;
Rem
Rem so the C's two condition codes are really three cases, and the split
Rem that matters is not which code was written but whether an argument is
Rem there to roll against. Always passes for free; Chance rolls, and the
Rem roll costs a draw from the generator even when it cannot fail -- a
Rem nocurmudgeons rule is exactly that, Chance(100), and skipping its draw
Rem puts every seeded game one behind. Naming the two apart is what keeps
Rem that from looking like a redundant test somebody could tidy away.
Type TravelCond = Always | Chance(Percent As Number) | Carrying(Obj As Number) | CarriedOrHere(Obj As Number) | NotInState(Obj As Number, State As Number)

Rem Where a row sends you: another room, a message and no movement, or
Rem one of the three hardcoded passages at main.c:765-870.
Type TravelDest = ToRoom(Room As Number) | ToMessage(Msg As Number) | ToSpecial(Which As Number)

Type Op
    Motion        As Number
    Condition     As TravelCond
    Destination   As TravelDest
    NoCurmudgeons As Boolean
    Stop          As Boolean

Def Travels() As List
    {
